Negative control — a successful edit resolves the SAVED address, not the
pre-edit one (review blocker B1).

Restores the raw-`IAddress` resolve on the manager's UPDATE limb: the
`.then(raw => raw ? mapAddress(raw) : undefined)` hop comes off, so `update`
resolves the API's snake_case body straight from `put()` while its `add:`
sibling keeps resolving a camelCase `Address` through `service.ensure`. This is
the exact asymmetry that shipped.

The mutant is invisible at every gate that measures shape. The PUT still goes
out, to the right url, with the right diff body; the server still persists the
change; the machine still reaches `processed` and the save still RESOLVES. Only
the resolved VALUE is wrong, and it is wrong silently: the shared machine's
`processing.updating.onDone` runs `setModel`, which re-parses through
`useModelParser(schema, data, baseModel)`; a raw body has no `address` key, so
`defaultsDeep(values, baseModel)` refills `address` wholesale from the
form-open snapshot, and the schema's `address: { $ref: "#/definitions/address" }`
carries no `properties`, so `safeValue` never recurses past the refilled value.
`context.model` reverts to the values the form opened with.

Declared subject — the B1 read-back. Open an EXISTING address, change one
`address` field (e.g. city London -> Paris), save, and assert on the value the
save RESOLVES and on `useContext().model`:
  - the resolved model's `address.city` is "Paris" (the saved value)
  - `useContext().model.address.city` is "Paris"
Under the mutant both are "London" — the form-open snapshot.

What does NOT discriminate, and is why this defect reached review with a green
suite:
  - asserting the PUT body / url / method — unchanged by the mutant
  - asserting the save resolved, or that the machine reached `processed`
  - asserting the collection refetched (AC-15) — invalidation is upstream of
    the resolve and fires either way
  - reading the saved city back off the COLLECTION rather than off the
    manager's own resolved value — the collection refetches from the server,
    which has Paris

Measured blast radius, suite at HEAD: 4 — the 2 declared subjects above plus 2
collateral (the `description` pair named below). Under the mutant the module
suite runs 116 passed / 5 failed: those 4 plus the standing AC-20/F6 halt,
which fails identically without it.

It was ZERO when this control was written (suite at be4b04cba, 115 passed with
only the AC-20/F6 halt red) — nothing in the suite guarded the behaviour, which
is how the defect reached review. The read-backs landed in 108a14f32 and
22944476c; the 4 above are that guard, measured.

Collateral, and NOT this control's declared subject: the two assertions on
`title` / `description` after a save. `setModel` derives the meta from the model
it just parsed, so a reverted model
reverts the meta with it. The meta's OWN control is the sibling mutant
`client-address.saved-meta.must-fail.patch`, which leaves the model correct and
drops only the derivation — a red there means the meta stopped being derived on
the save limbs; a red HERE means the model itself reverted.

This mutant restores BOTH halves of what shipped: the unmapped resolve on the
services adapter AND the `Promise<IAddress | undefined>` return type that
declared it, so the mutated tree still type-checks and a red can only come from
the read-back.

